 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Parameter Adjustment Constraint:** $\delta_r$ and $\delta_b$ are only allowed to decrease after a fully completed tree traversal has resulted in too high frame rates. They cannot be decreased mid-traversal [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Solid Node Normals:** Solid nodes do not have hierarchical normal information, so back-face culling must be deferred to the splat rendering stage using CSG probe normals [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Occlusion:** Hierarchical occlusion culling is not feasible for point-based primitives; reliance on hardware z-buffering is required [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Parameter Adjustment:** $\delta_r$ and $\delta_b$ are only allowed to decrease after a fully completed tree traversal has led to too high frame rates. This prevents oscillation during partial traversals [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Solid Node Normals:** Solid nodes lack hierarchical normal information. Back-face culling must be deferred to the splat rendering stage using the CSG probe, which adds overhead compared to Shell nodes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Occlusion:** Hierarchical occlusion culling is not implemented due to the point-based nature of the primitives. Relying on the hardware z-buffer is the only option, which may be less efficient for highly overlapping structures [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. The algorithm is adaptive, with traversal depth controlled by $\delta_r$ and $\delta_b$.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources. The tree is stored in breadth-first order to support progressive loading [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Fast recursive refinements due to breadth-first storage; effective culling of invisible branches; dynamic frame-rate control maintains real-time performance [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Hierarchical occlusion culling is not supported; back-face culling for Solid nodes is not hierarchical [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time rendering of large point-based volumetric datasets where frame-rate stability is critical [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When hierarchical occlusion culling is required for performance [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the tree depth and the selectivity of the culling tests. The adaptive frame-rate control ensures that the number of rendered splats is bounded by the performance target, effectively capping the rendering cost [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** The tree is stored breath-first, allowing progressive loading of only the parts of the data structure actually rendered during traversal [S1, unofficial]. Attributes are compressed and decompressed on-the-fly [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time performance is guaranteed through adaptive refinement. The use of bounding spheres and normal cones allows for efficient visibility culling [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Occlusion culling is limited to hardware z-buffering. Solid nodes require additional computation for normal derivation [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Museth and Lombeyna in the paper "TetSplat: Real-time Rendering and Volume Clipping of Large Volumetric Data Sets" (2004) [S1, unofficial]. It is part of the TetSplat rendering system, which uses hierarchical bounding-sphere trees (Shell and Solid) to represent volumetric data. The approach integrates visibility culling tests with adaptive volumetric clipping via CSG culling to allow dynamic exploration of interior parts of volumetric datasets [S1, unofficial].
+The method was introduced by Museth and Lombeyna in 2004 as part of the TetSplat system for real-time rendering and volume clipping of large volumetric datasets [S1, unofficial]. It was designed to handle both shell (surface) and solid (volume) representations efficiently by leveraging hierarchical tree structures and adaptive rendering techniques.
 
